{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/rem-torus-embeddable-completeness-remark","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Remark","doco:Section"],"label":"rem:torus-embeddable-completeness-remark","kind":"remark","title":"Number-theoretic nature of the completeness bound","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-torus-embeddable-complete"],"interprets":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-torus-embeddable-complete","text":"torus-embeddable-completeness-remark.md","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"rem:torus-embeddable-completeness-remark\"></a><p>This completeness stems from the Diophantine constraint <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(m-1)(n-1) \\leq 2g</code>. The Descartes value <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">g = 4</code> is the smallest genus accommodating all three lepton generations plus the required <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">m = 3</code> generators for the quark sector.</p>\n","source":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/brings-surface/torus-embeddable-completeness-remark.md","heading":"Remark","number":"7.12.8"}
